it was just amazing.

00:29:00.191 --> 00:29:02.879
Not only the students you can talk to and
learn from them, but

00:29:02.879 --> 00:29:05.420
you learn from the speakers
telling their life stories.

00:29:05.420 --> 00:29:07.970
So we had distressed investing guys.

00:29:07.970 --> 00:29:10.610
We had growth equity investors.

00:29:10.610 --> 00:29:12.200
We had venture capitalists.

00:29:12.200 --> 00:29:14.975
So we were able to see people
at the top of their field,

00:29:14.975 --> 00:29:17.250
how they thought differently.

00:29:17.250 --> 00:29:20.163
And that helped me figure
out where I fit in, too.

00:29:20.163 --> 00:29:23.777
&gt;&gt; That's awesome.

00:29:23.777 --> 00:29:28.953
All right, I'm going to start going

00:29:28.953 --> 00:29:35.370
to some of the questions that are in our
Q and A, keeping an eye on the time.

00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:38.413
So I'll read one from,
I think it's Michael.

00:29:38.413 --> 00:29:42.870
As international students,
are we required to apply to a specific

00:29:42.870 --> 00:29:47.160
program if we want to pursue
a career in PS at a US based firm?

00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:49.520
So this is a great actual question.

00:29:49.520 --> 00:29:53.464
We have two internationals on the panel
today, nd it comes up a lot of how much

00:29:53.464 --> 00:29:57.918
harder is it for international students to
get working authorization in the states.

00:29:57.918 --> 00:30:00.345
And if you can speak to you or
your friends,

00:30:00.345 --> 00:30:02.579
how they thought about that question?

00:30:02.579 --> 00:30:04.979
So Danielle, you want to start with that?

00:30:04.979 --> 00:30:10.100
&gt;&gt; Sure, so if y'all know that now the GSB
has the three year STEM extension,

00:30:10.100 --> 00:30:13.511
so if we want to stay in the US,
we can stay abroad for

00:30:13.511 --> 00:30:17.280
three years if we're working
in STEM related fields.

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:20.185
So that's a huge plus.

00:30:20.185 --> 00:30:26.127
And then it all depends on the firm, but
I would say that most investing firms,

00:30:26.127 --> 00:30:30.192
that have a good size,
they accept internationals.

00:30:30.192 --> 00:30:35.873
And my friends that are staying from Latin
America, they're staying in the US and

00:30:35.873 --> 00:30:42.062
doing investing, they'll have their visas,
those companies can sponsor their visa.

00:30:42.062 --> 00:30:47.227
So I think there's, maybe has to be
a boutique company that very few people,

00:30:47.227 --> 00:30:51.757
they have the admin or they just
don't want to hire international for

00:30:51.757 --> 00:30:55.431
some reason, but
I actually haven't heard about it.

00:30:55.431 --> 00:31:00.931
So I would say that for P, NBC, probably,
most of them will hire internationals.

00:31:02.041 --> 00:31:04.641
&gt;&gt; That's great, anything else,
Jessica, from your perspective?

00:31:06.350 --> 00:31:07.711
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so in my case,

00:31:07.711 --> 00:31:12.190
I was fortunate enough to get a green
card right when I started school.

00:31:12.190 --> 00:31:17.061
So I wasn't subject to sort of H-1B
international rules, but I was,

00:31:17.061 --> 00:31:18.370
when I was at Yale.

00:31:18.370 --> 00:31:21.188
And I think the H-1B, honestly again,

00:31:21.188 --> 00:31:25.304
I think a lot of the bigger
companies will sponsor an H-1B.

00:31:25.304 --> 00:31:26.506
But as Daniel said,

00:31:26.506 --> 00:31:31.550
there are many smaller boutique that are
starting to understand the H-1B better.

00:31:31.550 --> 00:31:34.046
I think the big problem a lot
of times with the H-1B,

00:31:34.046 --> 00:31:36.989
when you first graduate college,
so not for business school,

00:31:36.989 --> 00:31:40.440
when you first graduate college is
that you're in the regular pool.

00:31:40.440 --> 00:31:44.410
The regular pool is a much lower chance
of you getting the H-1B visa lottery.

00:31:44.410 --> 00:31:47.935
Once you finish your master's,
it's actually so much easier to get

00:31:47.935 --> 00:31:52.310
the lottery, because you double dip into,
you have a priority just for master's and

00:31:52.310 --> 00:31:56.198
then you double dip, because you can
also be eligible for the post college,

00:31:56.198 --> 00:31:58.660
sort of the just bachelor's lottery.

00:31:58.660 --> 00:32:03.384
And because the GSB gives the STEM
extension for three years, that means

00:32:03.384 --> 00:32:08.900
you're eligible, basically, after your
OPT for every year over the three years.

00:32:08.900 --> 00:32:13.571
So it's much easier to stay, and I think
As long as you are able to communicate

00:32:13.571 --> 00:32:17.597
that to companies, companies then feel
totally fine spending, basically,

00:32:17.597 --> 00:32:20.220
the investment to get you
to stay in the US because,

00:32:20.220 --> 00:32:23.640
obviously, you're GSB so
they want to keep you here.

00:32:23.640 --> 00:32:26.864
&gt;&gt; Awesome, the next question I
want to is from Enrique talk about

00:32:26.864 --> 00:32:30.770
because I've met a bunch of folks in
the audience have this question, as well.

00:32:30.770 --> 00:32:34.351
He was saying that starting a FinTech
company requires many skills.

00:32:34.351 --> 00:32:37.190
And we do know that, as I said,
about a third of folks coming in

00:32:37.190 --> 00:32:40.380
with finance backgrounds end
up going into operating roles.

00:32:40.380 --> 00:32:44.887
So, the question is, how does the GSB
program help you with these other skills,

00:32:44.887 --> 00:32:47.020
tech, product management, legal?

00:32:47.020 --> 00:32:48.690
What it's like to be an entrepreneur?

00:32:48.690 --> 00:32:52.172
So I'm going to look back to Ishan because
I know you founded your own company, and

00:32:52.172 --> 00:32:53.900
you know what it takes on both sides.

00:32:53.900 --> 00:32:56.680
You have them both in your career and
maybe you can speak to that.

00:32:58.120 --> 00:33:00.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and
I've invested in a lot of FinTechs too.

00:33:00.470 --> 00:33:05.240
So I can talk a bit about that, but
it all comes down to the people skills.

00:33:05.240 --> 00:33:08.740
Building a company is just a group of
people that believe the same thing.

00:33:08.740 --> 00:33:13.490
And it's those core skills at
GSB that you get to practice.

00:33:13.490 --> 00:33:17.640
You don't just learn from a textbook,
starting in the first quarter.

00:33:17.640 --> 00:33:21.610
That will be the most useful in
starting a company, in my mind.

00:33:21.610 --> 00:33:24.792
And, I think the idea will change,
most likely.

00:33:24.792 --> 00:33:27.941
But working that through
with your classmates,

00:33:27.941 --> 00:33:32.394
pitching them, there's even student
VC funds that you can pitch and

00:33:32.394 --> 00:33:36.290
get your ideas vetted from,
that's really valuable.

00:33:36.290 --> 00:33:41.758
But I would say, first and foremost
is finding co-founders, practicing

00:33:41.758 --> 00:33:47.422
what your pitch is, building a team,
and figuring out what your mission is.

00:33:47.422 --> 00:33:53.330
Which are all non-finance related things
are important for starting a company, so.

00:33:53.330 --> 00:33:55.400
&gt;&gt; And that's a great segue, Ishan.

00:33:55.400 --> 00:33:59.739
Many of our classes like startup garage or
Georgie talked about startup search fund,

00:33:59.739 --> 00:34:03.718
but startup garage or design for extreme
affordability, any of those classes

00:34:03.718 --> 00:34:08.140
have the student become, again,
the protagonist actually create a company.

00:34:08.140 --> 00:34:11.531
Anybody take one of those
outside of Georgie's class and

00:34:11.531 --> 00:34:13.374
talk about what that's like?

00:34:13.374 --> 00:34:15.580
Anybody here did startup garage?

00:34:15.580 --> 00:34:18.611
No, maybe I'll say a moment,
because all those things, Ishan,

00:34:18.611 --> 00:34:22.588
you were saying, I know you've done it
before, because you were the entrepreneur.

00:34:22.588 --> 00:34:26.673
But a group of about 46 students will come
together in a startup garage and they'll

00:34:26.673 --> 00:34:30.602
decide on what's the problem they want to
solve in order to graduate that course.

00:34:30.602 --> 00:34:33.840
The class action, the final class is,
what's the prototype?

00:34:33.840 --> 00:34:37.610
So, you really will get very hands-on
figuring out, who could be my co-founder?

00:34:37.610 --> 00:34:39.127
What's the problem we want to solve and

00:34:39.127 --> 00:34:41.320
how do we actually deliver
a product that will do that?

00:34:42.770 --> 00:34:44.598
Awesome, let's go to the next.

00:34:44.598 --> 00:34:49.207
Is there anybody else who thinks about how
you get entrepreneurial skills at Stanford

00:34:49.207 --> 00:34:54.085
or Daniel, you're launching, yeah, you're
actually building companies right now.

00:34:54.085 --> 00:34:55.295
What would you say?

00:34:55.295 --> 00:35:00.542
&gt;&gt; No, I would say like just the when
I think about my GSB experience,

00:35:00.542 --> 00:35:04.020
I think academics are maybe 20 to 25%.

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:07.740
I think just the things outside
of academics and formal classes,

00:35:07.740 --> 00:35:10.453
that's what the real experience is,
I think.

00:35:10.453 --> 00:35:13.611
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; So just every BBL every launch you can

00:35:13.611 --> 00:35:17.984
go to, hear from entrepreneurs,
hear from investors,

00:35:17.984 --> 00:35:22.520
you're learning there from them,
I started there.

00:35:22.520 --> 00:35:26.795
And then just the things that you
can do part-time, on the side,

00:35:26.795 --> 00:35:31.309
volunteering, part-time work,
doing projects with companies,

00:35:31.309 --> 00:35:36.790
that's where, and especially for
us during pandemic, that was huge.

00:35:36.790 --> 00:35:41.334
So, a little suggestion to anyone that
either wants to have the investing or

00:35:41.334 --> 00:35:45.240
entrepreneurial experiences,
try to do something on the side,

00:35:45.240 --> 00:35:46.890
some sort of project.

00:35:46.890 --> 00:35:52.310
And you will be amazed with your
staffer email saying your ideas.

00:35:52.310 --> 00:35:54.370
Why you're interested
in this sort of thing?

00:35:54.370 --> 00:35:57.018
Talking to an entrepreneur or
a certain firm, or a certain company,

00:35:57.018 --> 00:35:59.633
doing something with them,
that's where you will learn the most.

00:35:59.633 --> 00:36:03.888
And then combining that experience with
your actual classes that combination

00:36:03.888 --> 00:36:04.409
is huge.

00:36:04.409 --> 00:36:06.911
And even working with other classmates.

00:36:06.911 --> 00:36:11.471
And we have, I don't know, 20 classmates
that have already launched their own

00:36:11.471 --> 00:36:14.127
companies, some of them
during the pandemic.

00:36:14.127 --> 00:36:20.229
Just like you can do that,
you can do your on-the-side project and

00:36:20.229 --> 00:36:26.454
just working well starting I think
it's if you are able to manage it.

00:36:26.454 --> 00:36:30.259
I think is just the best
way to learn on the go and

00:36:30.259 --> 00:36:35.980
put all these entrepreneurial
experiences for your benefit.

00:36:35.980 --> 00:36:38.834
&gt;&gt; Awesome, and
I will just to cap what Daniel was saying,

00:36:38.834 --> 00:36:42.920
actually 18% of students launched
companies last year, which is amazing.

00:36:42.920 --> 00:36:46.130
So you're going to be surrounded by role
models who are really interested in that.

00:36:46.130 --> 00:36:46.680
And Poets and

00:36:46.680 --> 00:36:50.330
Funks did an article that looked back
over the last five graduating classes.

00:36:50.330 --> 00:36:52.117
So just go back over the last five years.

00:36:52.117 --> 00:36:56.425
And if you look at the top 100 students
from any business school who launched

00:36:56.425 --> 00:36:58.914
a company,
ours was almost half of the list.

00:36:58.914 --> 00:37:02.799
And we raised more money than all the
other ones put together like 1.6 billion,

00:37:02.799 --> 00:37:03.652
1.7 billion.

00:37:03.652 --> 00:37:07.295
And that's just people who are Ishan,
and Daniel, and Jessica, and

00:37:07.295 --> 00:37:10.212
Georgie's really stage of
career plus a couple years.

00:37:10.212 --> 00:37:13.170
So, you will be inspired
by the people around you.

00:37:13.170 --> 00:37:17.040
I have one more quick question from
Charles, what are some of the most

00:37:17.040 --> 00:37:20.859
effective ways to gain practical
investing experience at the GSB?

00:37:20.859 --> 00:37:24.307
And also, what are some of the avenues
available to students who want to raise

00:37:24.307 --> 00:37:25.700
money to start their own fund?

00:37:26.860 --> 00:37:29.857
So, I don't know,
who would like to answer that,

00:37:29.857 --> 00:37:32.949
ways to get investing experience or
raising money?

00:37:38.427 --> 00:37:40.993
&gt;&gt; I can jump in, to start off.

00:37:40.993 --> 00:37:45.109
So, I would say a good way to
get investing experience is,

00:37:45.109 --> 00:37:48.306
sorry, is the Finance and Investment Club.

00:37:48.306 --> 00:37:51.885
And this club is a group of
people who love investing.

00:37:51.885 --> 00:37:56.881
We have regular stock pitches, sorry,
I was one of the presidents, so

00:37:56.881 --> 00:37:58.525
we had stock pitches.

00:37:58.525 --> 00:38:02.991
We would have an investment committee
where people would pitch a stock, and

00:38:02.991 --> 00:38:05.989
then we would all sort of discuss,
poke holes, and

00:38:05.989 --> 00:38:09.680
kind of help refine that
person's investment thesis.

00:38:09.680 --> 00:38:13.410
And then we also ran
a pretty substantial fund.

00:38:13.410 --> 00:38:15.557
I forget exactly,
maybe Ishan you know, but

00:38:15.557 --> 00:38:20.130
I think it was over $300,000 where we
would actually test the students' ideas.

00:38:20.130 --> 00:38:23.180
And so that was a great
way to practice investing.

00:38:23.180 --> 00:38:26.791
Get feedback from your peers and
then also get feedback from the market.

00:38:26.791 --> 00:38:31.353
I would also say that sort of,
Ishan mentioned this in his comment too.

00:38:31.353 --> 00:38:35.470
There are a number of ways to get
practical experience while you're at

00:38:35.470 --> 00:38:40.520
school, given that we're in the heart of
Silicon Valley and right by San Francisco.

00:38:40.520 --> 00:38:43.942
There are so many people who
are looking to tap into GSB talent, and

00:38:43.942 --> 00:38:46.205
there are tons of
opportunity from alumni or

00:38:46.205 --> 00:38:50.323
people in the network to actually intern
during the year or do part-time work.

00:38:50.323 --> 00:38:53.214
And that's a great way to
learn from an investor and

00:38:53.214 --> 00:38:56.184
get practical experience
while you're at school.

00:38:56.184 --> 00:38:59.506
&gt;&gt; Awesome, great, now I'm going to
turn it back to the panelists.

00:38:59.506 --> 00:39:05.014
And if you could give some advice
to someone who's in the audience.

00:39:05.014 --> 00:39:07.612
What I say is, oftentimes,
people tell me, as the dean,

00:39:07.612 --> 00:39:10.010
that I won't get in,
I don't know if I should apply.

00:39:10.010 --> 00:39:11.431
And I'm always like, just do it,

00:39:11.431 --> 00:39:15.250
you're never going to know unless you put
yourself out there and do the application.

00:39:15.250 --> 00:39:18.583
So, for
someone who's thinking about applying,

00:39:18.583 --> 00:39:23.340
maybe even this fall, any tips you
would give them in that process?

00:39:23.340 --> 00:39:24.470
And I'll start with Jessica.

00:39:26.330 --> 00:39:31.437
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for sure, I think very much
underscoring that you should just apply,

00:39:31.437 --> 00:39:34.278
you can always apply again,
[LAUGH] if not.

00:39:34.278 --> 00:39:38.040
But I think a couple of things one is,
on preparing for

00:39:38.040 --> 00:39:42.820
the GMAT, obviously, is important,
but don't go crazy over it.

00:39:42.820 --> 00:39:47.934
I think that there's so much stress all
the time over the standardized tests and

00:39:47.934 --> 00:39:49.927
I really think that just like.

00:39:49.927 --> 00:39:54.211
Getting to know Kiersten and
getting to know sort of the GSB

00:39:54.211 --> 00:39:58.964
it's definitely a school that's
focused a lot on the holistic student.

00:39:58.964 --> 00:40:01.021
Obviously like not in the admissions but

00:40:01.021 --> 00:40:03.813
I definitely felt that when
I was doing my interviews.

00:40:03.813 --> 00:40:05.563
And once I got my admissions letter,

00:40:05.563 --> 00:40:09.357
one of the things that they were focusing
on was not the GMAT score a lot or my GPA.

00:40:09.357 --> 00:40:12.937
It was a lot around my experiences and
how I thought about my vision for

00:40:12.937 --> 00:40:14.291
what I wanted to do.

00:40:14.291 --> 00:40:16.492
I think the thing that I would suggest for

00:40:16.492 --> 00:40:19.728
people to keep in mind is think
about what the GSB motto is and

00:40:19.728 --> 00:40:23.906
what the GSB is trying to do in terms of
the leaders that they want to nurture.

00:40:23.906 --> 00:40:27.097
And how we want to really change
organizations, change lives,

00:40:27.097 --> 00:40:28.048
change the world.

00:40:28.048 --> 00:40:31.515
And think about very seriously
how you intend to do that and

00:40:31.515 --> 00:40:35.689
how you're going to use your MBA to
not just further your own career, but

00:40:35.689 --> 00:40:38.965
also how you really hope to
make an impact in the world.

00:40:38.965 --> 00:40:42.777
And thinking around that and I think
that really helps you become a lot more

00:40:42.777 --> 00:40:46.270
inspired to apply and
think about the impact you want to have.

00:40:46.270 --> 00:40:49.930
If you're not just worried about,
my God, I have to get this GMAT score.

00:40:49.930 --> 00:40:53.510
And so trying to think a little more about
the future and not just the short term,

00:40:53.510 --> 00:40:57.650
like how do I get through the application
process, I think, is important.

00:40:57.650 --> 00:40:59.130
&gt;&gt; Awesome, thanks Jessica.

00:40:59.130 --> 00:41:00.966
How about you Georgie, what would you say?

00:41:02.962 --> 00:41:05.550
&gt;&gt; I would definitely
echo Jessica's comments.

00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:10.559
And I think what I would say is that
the process can be incredibly daunting,

00:41:10.559 --> 00:41:14.851
but if you try to make the effort,
it actually, at least for me,

00:41:14.851 --> 00:41:19.965
was a process of great self-discovery,
especially the GSB application.

00:41:19.965 --> 00:41:24.739
One piece of advice I would give people
is often people try to game the system or

00:41:24.739 --> 00:41:28.055
feel that there's a correct
answer you have to give.

00:41:28.055 --> 00:41:31.591
And what I would say is definitely,
especially for the GSB app,

00:41:31.591 --> 00:41:35.100
give the answer that is
most authentic to you.

00:41:35.100 --> 00:41:39.185
I think that way you put out an
application that you can feel good about

00:41:39.185 --> 00:41:40.888
regardless of the outcome.

00:41:40.888 --> 00:41:44.773
And frankly, personally I'm sure I can say
it's probably more of what the GSB wants

00:41:44.773 --> 00:41:47.907
to see, wants people to be really
authentic and bring themselves.

00:41:47.907 --> 00:41:51.371
And so that's what I would say is
sometimes you have to tune out the noise

00:41:51.371 --> 00:41:55.126
and put out an application that you feel
is really true to yourself rather than

00:41:55.126 --> 00:41:58.070
what you think you should be or be saying.

00:41:58.070 --> 00:41:58.620
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:41:58.620 --> 00:42:02.166
And I remember Georgia you were
a classics major, is that right?

00:42:02.166 --> 00:42:04.608
And yeah, so many times-
&gt;&gt; Good memory.

00:42:04.608 --> 00:42:07.419
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah, I remember, I remember
your essays and everything, yeah.

00:42:07.419 --> 00:42:08.280
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:42:08.280 --> 00:42:12.764
&gt;&gt; But it's not about presenting
this finance person who's presenting

00:42:12.764 --> 00:42:15.970
Georgie and
who Georgie is what she cares about.

00:42:15.970 --> 00:42:17.830
So it's great advice.

00:42:17.830 --> 00:42:20.296
&gt;&gt; How about you Ishan,
what would you say?

00:42:20.296 --> 00:42:23.061
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I would echo everyone's points, but

00:42:23.061 --> 00:42:26.058
also add to take the writing
portion seriously.

00:42:26.058 --> 00:42:29.550
Try different drafts,
don't try to write it in one day.

00:42:29.550 --> 00:42:33.560
But also don't try to create
a shell of who you think you are.

00:42:33.560 --> 00:42:38.456
And I think that process of figuring out,
hey, this reflects who I am, and

00:42:38.456 --> 00:42:42.963
come back to it and see it again,
will help you realize a lot more about

00:42:42.963 --> 00:42:47.580
yourself and give you clarity even
before going to any MBA program.

00:42:47.580 --> 00:42:49.140
That's what happened to me.

00:42:49.140 --> 00:42:53.924
And so the exercise is valuable,
but yeah go ahead

00:42:53.924 --> 00:42:55.528
&gt;&gt; Ishan, I was going to say,

00:42:55.528 --> 00:42:58.280
is there anything you could speak to?

00:42:58.280 --> 00:43:01.616
because I bet a bunch of folks
are actually starting to write that essay,

00:43:01.616 --> 00:43:04.612
anything that you concretely did
to help you do some of that.

00:43:04.612 --> 00:43:08.831
You said you did multiple drafts, but-
&gt;&gt; I did multiple versions.

00:43:08.831 --> 00:43:13.336
So first I tried to answer the prompt
directly and simply, I remember.

00:43:13.336 --> 00:43:15.715
And then I read it the next day and
I said, hey,

00:43:15.715 --> 00:43:19.200
this sounds like what they want to hear,
but doesn't sound like me.

00:43:19.200 --> 00:43:24.233
So I'm just going to try to change it and
I started using more I statements,

00:43:24.233 --> 00:43:27.959
and things we learned at GSB,
but I didn't know yet.

00:43:27.959 --> 00:43:28.523
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That's great.

00:43:28.523 --> 00:43:33.032
&gt;&gt; And that was valuable, but I wasn't
sure if that's how I should submit a,

00:43:33.032 --> 00:43:37.270
quote unquote, professional school essay,
but I just went with it.

00:43:37.270 --> 00:43:40.860
And now I realized that's probably
the best way to go with it.

00:43:40.860 --> 00:43:45.286
Because I was admitted for
who I am, basically, and

00:43:45.286 --> 00:43:48.680
that's what makes leaders unique.

00:43:48.680 --> 00:43:53.070
And something I learned at GSB is
those parts that make us who we are.

00:43:53.070 --> 00:43:56.743
So letting yourself show through those,
through I statements and

00:43:56.743 --> 00:44:01.020
giving personal anecdotes and small
stories, I think is really important.

00:44:02.070 --> 00:44:05.542
Instead of just saying, hey,
this is who I am, this is why I'm perfect,

00:44:05.542 --> 00:44:06.720
because nobody's perfect.

00:44:08.190 --> 00:44:08.762
&gt;&gt; Well said.

00:44:08.762 --> 00:44:12.586
And we may be a little different
than other schools, so I want you to

00:44:12.586 --> 00:44:16.979
be careful to do your homework as
you're submitting other schools essays.

00:44:16.979 --> 00:44:20.565
But at the GSB I can promise you
we don't really care what you say

00:44:20.565 --> 00:44:24.380
is your aspiration in terms of
whether you're admitted or not.

00:44:24.380 --> 00:44:27.658
So if you've never been in finance like
Jessica and you say I want to go into

00:44:27.658 --> 00:44:30.830
venture capital, we're not going to say,
she could never do it.

00:44:30.830 --> 00:44:34.090
We really believe that if you come
to the GSB with the community and

00:44:34.090 --> 00:44:38.002
with all that you'll bring here, you will
find a way to meet your aspiration to

00:44:38.002 --> 00:44:39.740
fulfill your dreams as they were.

00:44:39.740 --> 00:44:42.908
It may not be immediately,
it may be a pivot down the road,

00:44:42.908 --> 00:44:44.600
but we know you'll get there.

00:44:44.600 --> 00:44:49.119
So feel free to just speak to what you
really think will be your true North and

00:44:49.119 --> 00:44:51.580
we know that's going to evolve over time.

00:44:51.580 --> 00:44:54.849
But at this point in time, is there
something, some problem you want to solve,

00:44:54.849 --> 00:44:57.930
something you care about, something
you want to work on as an aspiration.

00:44:57.930 --> 00:45:00.030
That's really what we want to know.

00:45:00.030 --> 00:45:02.091
So Daniel, how about you?

00:45:02.091 --> 00:45:04.724
&gt;&gt; I like everything that's been said.

00:45:04.724 --> 00:45:08.194
I think authenticity just
is very important and

00:45:08.194 --> 00:45:10.458
just think why are you unique.

00:45:10.458 --> 00:45:12.056
What makes you unique?

00:45:12.056 --> 00:45:13.888
And we're all unique, so

00:45:13.888 --> 00:45:19.420
really tell me what makes you unique
versus anyone else that is applying.

00:45:19.420 --> 00:45:23.935
And normally, the professional side,
we all kind of go to companies,

00:45:23.935 --> 00:45:26.343
work in company different roles, but

00:45:26.343 --> 00:45:30.770
normally the unique stuff are in
the most on the personal side.

00:45:30.770 --> 00:45:33.971
And that's where I think
where the more emotions and

00:45:33.971 --> 00:45:37.620
more powerful story comes when
you kind of reflect on that.

00:45:37.620 --> 00:45:42.483
Normally, the things that have happened to
you and that have impacted in your life

00:45:42.483 --> 00:45:46.150
mostly come on those personal or
non-professional things,

00:45:46.150 --> 00:45:49.692
that maybe some of them are also
in the professional stuff.

00:45:49.692 --> 00:45:54.307
You can maybe complement, but just
reflecting on really what drives you and

00:45:54.307 --> 00:45:57.567
what matters most and
what you want to do in the future.

00:45:57.567 --> 00:46:02.650
What are the things you want to work on
during this two year experience, and just

00:46:02.650 --> 00:46:08.210
trying to enjoy that process, not seeing
it as this is an essay I have to write.

00:46:08.210 --> 00:46:10.340
This is a reflection process.

00:46:10.340 --> 00:46:15.520
I'm given the opportunity to reflect about
my life and why I want to go to this.

00:46:15.520 --> 00:46:19.810
This is not an MBA, this is a leadership
and personal development program.

00:46:19.810 --> 00:46:24.502
It's an opportunity to be more self aware,
more introspective, to learn from

00:46:24.502 --> 00:46:29.650
diversity, from others and be able to
also support others in their journeys.

00:46:29.650 --> 00:46:32.376
So just understanding what
the GSB experience is about,

00:46:32.376 --> 00:46:35.020
which really that's what
makes this school unique.

00:46:35.020 --> 00:46:37.462
This is not an MBA,
it's a personal leadership,

00:46:37.462 --> 00:46:40.675
that's what I think is the most
unique thing about this program.

00:46:40.675 --> 00:46:43.235
So once you read, talk with alumni,
talk with students,

00:46:43.235 --> 00:46:44.870
read the articles that GSB puts out.

00:46:44.870 --> 00:46:47.762
Read about the classes and
you understand those values and

00:46:47.762 --> 00:46:50.476
you read about the values and
the ethos of the school and

00:46:50.476 --> 00:46:53.501
you understand that that's
what makes this school unique.

00:46:53.501 --> 00:46:56.411
That's where you maybe you
start relaxing and say,

00:46:56.411 --> 00:47:01.041
well, like let's connect on that and let's
see what are the things that connect me

00:47:01.041 --> 00:47:03.771
to those things that the GSB
say they care about.

00:47:03.771 --> 00:47:08.083
Because the values and the things
Christine talks about in the articles she

00:47:08.083 --> 00:47:13.026
writes and in the interviews that has been
published about them, that that's true.

00:47:13.026 --> 00:47:16.797
It's about that, it's not about
the GMAT and GPA and all this stuff.

00:47:16.797 --> 00:47:21.728
There's many people with that and a lot of
those high grades and stuff don't get in,

00:47:21.728 --> 00:47:26.173
and people like me that got 60 points
below the average or 80 points below

00:47:26.173 --> 00:47:30.590
the average in the GMAT got in maybe
because there's a story around that.

00:47:30.590 --> 00:47:32.750
And just to highlight the GMAT aspect,

00:47:32.750 --> 00:47:36.535
I almost didn't apply to the GSB,
almost didn't apply.

00:47:36.535 --> 00:47:41.075
A lot of my friends didn't apply because
they got lower than average GMATs, and

00:47:41.075 --> 00:47:42.585
that's incredibly sad.

00:47:42.585 --> 00:47:46.416
Because this school looks for
that thing that makes you special, and

00:47:46.416 --> 00:47:48.541
that's your leadership potential.

00:47:48.541 --> 00:47:52.346
And that GMAT normally,
it like a GMAT is a score, it's an exam.

00:47:52.346 --> 00:47:55.450
That doesn't define your
leadership potential.

00:47:55.450 --> 00:47:59.689
So I'll encourage everyone to please
apply and please be authentic and

00:47:59.689 --> 00:48:01.527
just tell your unique story and

00:48:01.527 --> 00:48:06.137
just enjoy this reflection process of
going throughout through this essay.

00:48:06.137 --> 00:48:07.370
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:48:07.370 --> 00:48:10.070
I'm looking,
we have just two more minutes.

00:48:10.070 --> 00:48:11.653
That was great tips and

00:48:11.653 --> 00:48:17.165
one thing I realized we haven't talked
a lot about directly is the community.

00:48:17.165 --> 00:48:21.334
So anybody want to speak to what surprised
the most because I always say we're like

00:48:21.334 --> 00:48:23.169
a family and people support you.

00:48:23.169 --> 00:48:26.663
But coming from me, I think it's a little
hard to believe at times, [LAUGH] so

00:48:26.663 --> 00:48:28.097
you guys have experienced it.

00:48:28.097 --> 00:48:30.394
But anyone want to put their hand up and

00:48:30.394 --> 00:48:35.226
talk about what surprised the most about
becoming part of this, TSP family?

00:48:39.401 --> 00:48:40.643
Do all volunteer one?

00:48:40.643 --> 00:48:42.500
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I can do a quick.

00:48:42.500 --> 00:48:43.905
&gt;&gt; There's your hand, thanks.

00:48:43.905 --> 00:48:49.517
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
we have a special portal for alumni.

00:48:49.517 --> 00:48:53.885
And so I reached out during my
first year for six months, keynote,

00:48:53.885 --> 00:48:55.921
maybe 20 different alumni.

00:48:55.921 --> 00:48:59.965
And I think the average response
was probably half a day,

00:48:59.965 --> 00:49:03.683
regardless of role,
responsibility, function.

00:49:03.683 --> 00:49:07.807
So the network the community is so
tight knit, it's pretty incredible.

00:49:07.807 --> 00:49:12.381
And as alumni,
I think we feel an obligation to support

00:49:12.381 --> 00:49:17.263
those that are coming after us and
support the community.

00:49:17.263 --> 00:49:21.808
I saw that firsthand, I think I'm still
about to see that the benefits and

00:49:21.808 --> 00:49:24.537
the fruits of that, but it's really true.

00:49:24.537 --> 00:49:29.138
And I think you're right, Kirsten to say
that it has to come from the students.

00:49:29.138 --> 00:49:32.001
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; because I didn't really believe it

00:49:32.001 --> 00:49:34.179
until I saw it, so like cool stuff.

00:49:34.179 --> 00:49:37.718
&gt;&gt; So awesome,
anybody else want to, Jessica?

00:49:37.718 --> 00:49:40.908
&gt;&gt; Yeah, happy to jump in sort of two
points that I think are interesting.

00:49:40.908 --> 00:49:45.941
One is to just double-click on what
Sean said, as a great example.

00:49:45.941 --> 00:49:47.684
I remember when I got into the GSB and

00:49:47.684 --> 00:49:50.078
I wanted to explore a career
in venture capital.

00:49:50.078 --> 00:49:53.179
I decided that I was
going to go on LinkedIn and

00:49:53.179 --> 00:49:57.501
I searched general partner
plus Stanford GSB on LinkedIn.

00:49:57.501 --> 00:50:02.317
And I reached out to approximately
45 people saying that

00:50:02.317 --> 00:50:04.489
I had just been admitted.

00:50:04.489 --> 00:50:08.488
So just to start in the fall, gave them my
background will love to talk to them for

00:50:08.488 --> 00:50:11.437
like 20 minutes or get a coffee,
whatever they wanted.

00:50:11.437 --> 00:50:15.353
Just to learn a little more and
get to understand VC imagining, of course,

00:50:15.353 --> 00:50:18.791
that maybe five people would reply
to me and so that would be fine.

00:50:18.791 --> 00:50:22.109
It turns out that every single one of
the 45 people replied and were like,

00:50:22.109 --> 00:50:25.374
absolutely, I'll meet with you come for
coffee and no come to my office,

00:50:25.374 --> 00:50:26.705
it was really enthusiastic.

00:50:26.705 --> 00:50:29.515
So I actually ended up taking
a week off from my job because so

00:50:29.515 --> 00:50:33.285
many people have replied and we're excited
to meet you that I did like a tour of,

00:50:33.285 --> 00:50:34.367
[LAUGH] Sand Hill Road.

00:50:34.367 --> 00:50:39.304
And was going to all of these offices
into coffee at coupon coffee or whatever.

00:50:39.304 --> 00:50:41.337
Because everyone was so
excited to talk to me.

00:50:41.337 --> 00:50:46.469
And I think that that was already like
a very, very clear example of how

00:50:46.469 --> 00:50:51.350
much the alums are there for you and
how tight the GSB community is.

00:50:51.350 --> 00:50:56.445
And I think that's also true at
the GSB while you're a student

00:50:56.445 --> 00:51:02.426
just the level of support and
love that exists between all the students.

00:51:02.426 --> 00:51:06.794
So many people that say you had
a difficult family emergency,

00:51:06.794 --> 00:51:09.404
which happened to me during school.

00:51:09.404 --> 00:51:12.483
Just everyone will rise up to
the occasion, and support you, and

00:51:12.483 --> 00:51:15.353
find ways to help you, and
show up with bagels at your house.

00:51:15.353 --> 00:51:20.440
And it's just really such an incredible
community and it's really unparalleled

00:51:20.440 --> 00:51:24.727
to anything I've ever seen in my
GSB classmates are my best friends.

00:51:24.727 --> 00:51:26.096
And I know they'll be my best friends for
life.

00:51:26.096 --> 00:51:29.795
So I think both on like a personal level
and also on the professional level,

00:51:29.795 --> 00:51:31.686
it's really a tight knit community.

00:51:31.686 --> 00:51:34.259
&gt;&gt; Awesome, well,
that's a great comment to wrap up on.

00:51:34.259 --> 00:51:39.144
I want to thank you so much Jessica,
Daniel, Sean, Georgie.

00:51:39.144 --> 00:51:42.387
It's so fun to see you after reading all
of your applications live on the screen.

00:51:42.387 --> 00:51:45.281
So I really appreciate your support and
for those of you in the audience,

00:51:45.281 --> 00:51:46.330
thank you for joining us.

00:51:46.330 --> 00:51:49.556
And good luck if you're putting in
an application we hope to get to know you

00:51:49.556 --> 00:51:50.190
better soon.

00:51:50.190 --> 00:51:51.107
Have a great day.

00:51:51.107 --> 00:51:53.630
[MUSIC]

